<p>Hello! I'm Bart, an hobby photographer with a passion for nature, light microscopy and imaging techniques. I started with photography through bird watching in 2024, when I purchased my first DLSR camera. It changes the way you look at nature, with the constant excitement of looming wildlife encounters and the challenge of capturing it on camera. Moreover, it makes you more ware of habitats, seasons and animal behavior, which add depth to being out in nature.</p>

<p>My physics background in (super-resolution) microscopy has left me with a soft spot for the technical side of photography. It has me regularly browsing websites like The Digital Picture, DXOMark, and PhotonsToPhotos to compare cameras and lenses, reading up on image processing, and watching videos on getting the most sharpness out of a shot. Combined with a booming availability of second hand gear, it's easy to lose oneself in this hobby. After an initial buying spree, I decided to first work on my technique before upgrading further. I learned especially that understanding how to use the available light and perspective brings out the best colors and contrast in a photo.</p>

<p>At home I also have an old Olympus microscope from the 80's that I modernized with darkfield, a full frame digital camera and a motorized focusing stage to take micrographs. Here also I find it fun to work on the technical side and as a results I haven't captured many images with it yet. Also because I find it more challenging to get proper subject matter (when zoomed in there is so much unintersting stuff too!).</p>



